# 1. Worst Possible Idea

We each prepared an initial set with AI assistance. These entries combine the written notes and keep the author of each starting point visible. They are not presented as a transcript of a live team conversation.

### H-W1 — A Client Form Nobody Finishes

**Starting contribution:** Handong He

**Problem:** A web studio needs descriptions, images, and answers from a client before it can finish the site.

**Bad idea:** Require a 200-question form to be completed in one sitting.

**Make it worse:** Delete unfinished answers when the client closes the page, use unexplained marketing terms, and keep sending reminders after questions have been answered.

**Useful flip:** Start from material the client already has, ask short questions about the gaps, and organize the result around the site's pages.

**Related concept:** `H-A1 ClientReady`

**Question to test:** A simpler process may not help if the client does not consider the project a priority.

---

### H-W2 — Every File Is Called Final

**Starting contribution:** Handong He

**Problem:** A freelancer receives feedback on several versions and cannot tell which instructions still apply.

**Bad idea:** Name every version final and collect comments in disappearing messages.

**Make it worse:** Mix comments from different drafts, leave conflicting instructions unresolved, and count silence as approval.

**Useful flip:** Attach feedback and explicit approval to a particular version, with unresolved decisions still visible.

**Related concept:** `H-A4 Version approval page`

**Question to test:** This may be useful inside another product without justifying a separate subscription.

---

### H-W3 — An Interview Coach That Invents Your Project

**Starting contribution:** Handong He

**Problem:** A candidate needs to explain their own technical contribution under follow-up questions.

**Bad idea:** Write an impressive answer about a project the candidate never built.

**Make it worse:** Invent performance numbers and technology choices, then repeat the original answer whenever the interviewer asks for detail.

**Useful flip:** Practice from a real project: what the candidate did, why they made a decision, and what went wrong. Follow up on unclear answers.

**Related concept:** `H-B1 ProjectProof`

**Question to test:** Feedback needs to separate an unclear explanation from a genuine gap in technical understanding.

---

### H-W4 — A Mock Interview After the Real Interview

**Starting contribution:** Handong He

**Problem:** A candidate needs timely practice and useful feedback before an interview.

**Bad idea:** Schedule the practice session for the following day and give only a numerical score.

**Make it worse:** Ask unrelated questions, give contradictory advice, and delete the recording.

**Useful flip:** Offer a short session before the interview, identify specific improvements, and let the candidate retry difficult questions. A peer can provide the feedback.

**Related concept:** `H-B7 Peer interview exchange`

**Question to test:** A partner must be available, show up, and give useful feedback.

---

### M-W1 — A Model That Reads Everything Twice

**Starting contribution:** Mansi Pravinkumar Rank

**Problem:** A document pipeline may use costly processing even when a simpler route would meet the task's needs.

**Bad idea:** Send every page, including blank cover sheets, through the most expensive available vision model.

**Make it worse:** Charge for unhelpful outputs and retry endlessly without checking whether a simpler method would work.

**Useful flip:** Try local text extraction or OCR first, and escalate when a measured quality requirement calls for it.

**Related concept:** `M-A1 Strata`

**Question to test:** The savings must matter to a reachable customer and must survive a quality comparison.

---

### M-W2 — A Bill Nobody Can Explain

**Starting contribution:** Mansi Pravinkumar Rank

**Problem:** A team cannot connect document-processing charges to the work that was actually required.

**Bad idea:** Charge one opaque rate for every document, regardless of its content or processing route.

**Make it worse:** Change the rate unpredictably at renewal and leave nobody able to explain how it was set.

**Useful flip:** Show the processing route and associated cost so customers can understand what they are paying for.

**Related concept:** `M-A2 Spend visibility and pricing within M-A1 Strata`

**Question to test:** This looks more like a feature or pricing choice within Strata than an independent business.

---

### M-W3 — An Editor That Forgets You Exist

**Starting contribution:** Mansi Pravinkumar Rank

**Problem:** A creator may need to repeat or correct editing preferences across projects.

**Bad idea:** Ignore every previous project and ask the creator to explain their taste from the beginning each time.

**Make it worse:** Make the creator type a detailed style description, then ignore much of it.

**Useful flip:** Build an editable preference profile from the creator's own completed work and use it to guide later edits.

**Related concept:** `M-B1 Auteur`

**Question to test:** Saved instructions and brand templates already exist. The inferred profile must improve results beyond those options.

---

### M-W4 — A Cut That Ships Without Checking

**Starting contribution:** Mansi Pravinkumar Rank

**Problem:** A creator can discover a style mismatch after spending time polishing a video.

**Bad idea:** Finish and export the edit without comparing it with the creator's preferences.

**Make it worse:** Choose unsuitable music and provide no opportunity to review the mismatch before export.

**Useful flip:** Compare a rough cut with the creator's reviewed profile early enough to guide changes.

**Related concept:** `M-B3 Early rough-cut check within M-B1 Auteur`

**Question to test:** This is likely a feature within Auteur. Its usefulness depends on whether creators agree with the feedback.

